// `a | b | c` in one instance — and the first consumer `Proc.run` has had.
//
// `exec.wac` beside this is the *other* half of running something: across a spawn, where a
// capability is not a value and what crosses is a description of one. This is the half that stays
// inside the instance, which is what a browser tab has and what `packages/sh` calls its sequential
// path.
//
// ## The shipped sequential pipeline buffers, and its own comment is the bug report
//
// `packages/sh/src/exec.wac`'s `runStages` carries `u8[] carried` from each stage to the next — one
// stage runs to completion, its whole output is held, the next one starts. The comment underneath
// says what that costs:
//
// > Here a stage that overflowed what this shell will hold produces no bytes, so the stage after it
// > counts an empty input and reports success: `seq 1 1500000 | wc -c` printed `0` with status 0,
// > and `n=$(…)` captured a plausible number with the reason on a stream no script reads. Carrying
// > on would be computing an answer from nothing.
//
// A wrong answer with status 0, and the fix in the tree is a `refusedStage` flag that ends the
// pipeline early — a mechanism whose entire job is to notice that the buffer overflowed.
//
// ## And `Proc.run` reproduces it, which is this file's finding
//
// The projections audit found `pushChild`/`popChild` was a paired protocol over hidden state and
// gave it a value: `Proc.run(args, stdin, cwd)` answering a `Captured`. That fixes the *pairing* and
// keeps the *buffering* — `Captured { Bytes out; Bytes err; i32 status; }` is the whole output in
// memory, exactly what `carried` is, and the shipped `Captured` already has a `truncated` field and
// an 8 MiB cap because somebody hit this.
//
// **It is also inconsistent with the same audit's other answer.** `Files.open` answers an
// `AsyncGenerator<Bytes, …>` and `Files.create` answers a `Sink`, both because a stream is what a
// caller wants; `Proc.run` answers a buffer three fields wide. One audit, two shapes, and the
// pipeline is the case where the difference is a wrong answer rather than a preference.
//
// So the signature below is what `Proc.run` should have been, and `vision/std` has been changed to
// match rather than this file working around it.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec, AsyncGenerator } from "core";
import { Out, Proc, Exit, NotGranted } from "std";

/** One stage: a program and its arguments. */
export struct Stage { Vec<Bytes> argv; }

/**
 * A pipeline, as a fold.
 *
 * Each stage takes the previous stage's output *as a stream* and answers its own, so the whole
 * pipeline is one expression and nothing between two stages is ever held whole. `seq 1 1500000 |
 * wc -c` counts fifteen hundred thousand lines with a buffer the size of one chunk.
 *
 * **Lazy, which is the half buffering cannot do at all.** `cat big | head -1` pulls one chunk
 * through `cat` and stops, because `head` stops asking. The shipped sequential path runs `cat` to
 * the end first, and the streaming path only avoids that by having a kernel hold the pipe.
 */
export async Result<Exit, NotGranted> pipeline(
  Proc proc, Out out, Vec<Stage> stages, AsyncGenerator<Bytes, Exit> stdin, string cwd
) {
  AsyncGenerator<Bytes, Exit> flowing = stdin;
  Vec<Exit> ends = Vec.create();

  for (Stage s in stages.items()) {
    match (proc.run(s.argv, flowing, cwd)) {
      Ok(next): { flowing = next; }
      Err(e):   { return Err(e); }
    }
  }

  // The last stage's output is the pipeline's, and draining it is what makes every earlier stage
  // run at all — nothing above has executed yet.
  try await for (Bytes chunk in flowing) { _ = out.write(chunk); }
  return Ok(lastOf(ends));
}

Exit lastOf(Vec<Exit> ends) { … }

// ## 1. A pipeline's status is every stage's, and a generator returns one value
//
// `flowing` is rebuilt each time round the loop, so by the end only the **last** stage's return is
// reachable — and bash's rule is the last stage's status, so that reads like a match. It is not.
//
// `packages/sh/src/refusal.wac` is a whole file about why:
//
// > bash's rule for a pipeline's status — the last stage's — quietly … `wc` then counts an empty
// > input, and the pipeline reports **0 with status 0**.
//
// A stage that failed in the middle has to reach the shell, and here its `Exit` is inside a
// generator nobody holds any more. `ends` above is written and never filled, because there is no
// form for *keep a handle on the return of a generator I have already piped into something else*.
// Awaiting it would deadlock: the earlier stage does not finish until the later one has drained it.
//
// **`$PIPESTATUS` is the shell feature this is**, and it is not a shell question — it is *what a
// caller can learn from a generator it has handed to another generator*. `../../QUESTIONS.md` has
// *whether `try await for` binds the return at all*; this is the same seam with more than one
// producer, which that entry does not consider.
//
// ## 2. Abandoning a generator is unspecified, and laziness is what makes it happen
//
// `cat big | head -1` stops pulling after one chunk, so `cat`'s generator is suspended forever with
// a file open. The shipped *sequential* pipeline never has this, because a buffered stage always runs
// to completion — laziness is what introduces it, and laziness is the reason to want streams.
//
// **The shipped streaming pipeline does have it, and solves it the opposite way.** `runPipeline`:
//
// > `seq 1 200000 | head -1` used to take twelve seconds, because each stage ran to completion and
// > handed its whole output to the next. A real shell returns at once: **`head` closing its input is
// > what stops `seq`.** The difference is invisible for small inputs and total for endless ones —
// > `yes | head` only ended at all because a queue nobody reads is capped at 8 MiB. Issue 0038.
//
// So the mechanism that stops the producer is an **explicit close travelling upstream**, and this
// file replaced it with the *absence* of a pull. That is the same trade `Socket.closeSend` is about
// one layer down — *"say that is all without saying goodbye"* — and the shipped design chose a
// positive signal in both places.
//
// Which reframes finding 2 rather than answering it. It is not only *what happens to an abandoned
// generator*; it is **whether a consumer should have to say it is done**, when saying so is a thing
// the tree already has a call for and a bug report behind. An absence cannot carry a reason, cannot
// be distinguished from a slow consumer, and — the part that matters here — cannot reach past one
// link: `head` closing its input stops `seq` through however many stages are between them, and a
// consumer that merely stops pulling stops the stage next to it and nothing further.
//
// Three things nobody has said: whether an abandoned generator's `defer` runs, whether it is
// distinguishable from one that finished, and whether the *file* it holds is closed. The first is
// `../../QUESTIONS.md`'s trap-and-scope entry arriving a third way; the third is worse, because
// `Files.open` answers a generator and closing is what its `Sink` counterpart has an explicit method
// for. A source has no `close` and a sink does.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **A stage that is a builtin.** `echo a | cat` has no program to run for `echo` — a shell's
// builtins are functions in the shell. `proc.run` takes an argv, so a builtin needs a second path,
// which is what the shipped `runCommand` is. Making a builtin an `AsyncGenerator<Bytes, Exit>` too
// would unify them and is a change to what a builtin *is*, not to this file.
//
// **The subshell rule.** bash runs every stage of a real pipeline in a subshell, so `v=b | cat`
// must not assign in the parent — `issues/system/0114` is that bug in this tree. `Proc.run` has no
// grants argument and shares the instance's authority by design, so there is nothing here that
// isolates a stage's *variables*; the shipped code forks a `Shell` value to get it. That is a shell
// concern rather than a capability one, and it is the one place where *the child is the same
// instance* stops being merely honest and starts being a missing feature.
